      <exercise label="CP-2dice">
          <title>Roll Two Dice</title>
          <introduction>
              <p>
                You roll two fair dice. The sample space for this experiment is shown below.
                 
             </p>
             <image source="Images/Roll-Two-Dice.png" width="60%"/>
          </introduction>

            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        What is the probability that at least one of two dice lands on the number 4, <em>given</em> that the sum of the two dice is 9? 
                    </p>
                </statement>
            
          <choices randomize="yes" multiple-correct="yes">
              <choice correct="yes">
                  <statement>
                       <p>
                          <m> 0.500 </m>
                      </p>
                   </statement>

                  <feedback>
                      <p>
                          Correct! 
                      </p>
                 </feedback>
             </choice>

             <choice>
                 <statement>
                       <p>
                          <m> 0.056 </m>
                       </p>
                 </statement>

                 <feedback>
                      <p> </p>
                 </feedback>
              </choice>

             <choice>
                 <statement>
                       <p>
                          <m> 0.111 </m>
                       </p>
                 </statement>

                 <feedback>
                      <p> </p>
                 </feedback>
              </choice>

             <choice>
                 <statement>
                       <p>
                          <m> 0.306 </m>
                       </p>
                 </statement>

                 <feedback>
                      <p> </p>
                 </feedback>
              </choice>

              <choice>
                 <statement>
                       <p>
                          <m> 0.944 </m>
                       </p>
                 </statement>

                 <feedback>
                      <p> </p>
                 </feedback>
              </choice>
          </choices>

        </task>

        <task>

           <statement>
                <p>
                   What is the probability that the sum of the two dice is 7, <em>given</em> that at least one of two dice lands on the number 2? Write your answer as a decimal, rounded to three decimal places.
               </p>

               <p>
                  <fillin width="8" answer="0.182"/>
               </p>
           </statement>
  
           <evaluation>
              <evaluate>
                 <test correct="yes">
                     <numcmp use-answer="yes"/>
                     <feedback>
                         <p>
                             Correct! 
                         </p>
                     </feedback>
                  </test>

                  <test>
                     <numcmp use-answer="yes" tolerance="0.01"/>
                     <feedback>
                         <p>
                             Your answer is within 0.01 of the value wanted.
                         </p>
                     </feedback>
                  </test>
                </evaluate>

            </evaluation>
            </task>
      </exercise>



   <exercise xml:id="CP-generic-practice-A" >
     <title>Formula/Rule Practice</title>
         <statement>
          <p>
             If <m>P(E \cap F)=0.024</m>, <m>P(E | F)=0.06</m>, and <m>P(F| E)=0.10</m>, then match the following probabilities to their values.
             </p>
         </statement>

         <feedback>
             <p>
                 Add later.
              </p>
         </feedback>

         <cardsort>
                <match>
                    <premise order="1"><m> P(E)</m></premise>
                    <response>0.24</response>
                </match>
                <match>
                    <premise order="1"><m> P(F)</m></premise>
                    <response>0.40</response>
                </match>
                <match>
                    <premise order="1"><m> P(E \cup F)</m></premise>
                    <response>0.62</response>
                </match>
                <match>
                    <response>0.64</response>
                </match>
         </cardsort>

         
        </exercise>


    <exercise xml:id="CP-independence" >
      <title>Independence</title>
      <introduction>
          <p>
             Suppose that <m>A</m> and <m>B</m> are two independent events for which <m>P(A)=0.2</m> and <m>P(B)=0.56</m>. 
          </p>
      </introduction>
    
      <task>

           <statement>
                <p>
                  Find <m>P(A|B)</m>. Write your answer as a decimal, rounded to three decimal places.
               </p>

               <p>
                  <fillin width="8" answer="0.560"/>
               </p>
           </statement>
  
           <evaluation>
              <evaluate>
                 <test correct="yes">
                     <numcmp use-answer="yes"/>
                     <feedback>
                         <p>
                             Correct! The probability of B does not depend on whether A has occurred.
                         </p>
                     </feedback>
                  </test>             
              </evaluate>
            </evaluation>
      </task>

      <task>

           <statement>
                <p>
                  Find <m>P(A \cap B)</m>. Write your answer as a decimal, rounded to three decimal places.
               </p>

               <p>
                  <fillin width="8" answer="0.112"/>
               </p>
           </statement>
  
           <evaluation>
              <evaluate>
                 <test correct="yes">
                     <numcmp use-answer="yes"/>
                     <feedback>
                         <p>
                             Correct! When events are independent you can multiply them together to find the probability of their intersection.
                         </p>
                     </feedback>
                  </test>             
              </evaluate>
            </evaluation>
      </task>

      <task>

           <statement>
                <p>
                  Find <m>P(A\cup B)</m>. Write your answer as a decimal, rounded to three decimal places.
               </p>

               <p>
                  <fillin width="8" answer="0.648"/>
               </p>
           </statement>
  
           <evaluation>
              <evaluate>
                 <test correct="yes">
                     <numcmp use-answer="yes"/>
                     <feedback>
                         <p>
                             Correct! Independent <m>\neq</m> Disjoint or Mutually Exclusive.
                         </p>
                     </feedback>
                  </test>             
              </evaluate>
            </evaluation>
            </task>
      </exercise>




<exercise xml:id="ex-server-filter-bayes">
    <title>Smart Filter Diagnostics and Bayes' Theorem</title>
    <statement>
        <p>
            An automated campus server uses a machine learning filter to scan incoming files. Let <m>A</m> be the event that a file is malicious software, and let <m>B</m> be the event that the filter flags the file as a threat. The system analytics provide the following base probabilities:
            <me>P(A) = 0.44, \quad P(B \mid A) = 0.03, \quad P(B^c \mid A^c) = 0.06</me>
            If a randomly arriving file is flagged as a threat by the filter, what is the posterior probability <m>P(A \mid B)</m> that it is actually a malicious software file?
        </p>
    </statement>
    <choices randomize="yes">
        <choice correct="yes">
            <statement>
                <m>0.0245</m> 
            </statement>
            <feedback>
                <p>
                    Correct! First, find the complements: <m>P(A^c) = 1 - 0.44 = 0.56</m> and <m>P(B \mid A^c) = 1 - 0.06 = 0.94</m>. Next, apply the Law of Total Probability to find the denominator: <m>P(B) = (0.03)(0.44) + (0.94)(0.56) = 0.0132 + 0.5264 = 0.5396</m>. Finally, apply Bayes' Theorem: <m>P(A \mid B) = \frac{0.0132}{0.5396} \approx 0.0245</m>.
                </p>
            </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
            <statement>
                <m>0.5396</m>
            </statement>
            <feedback>
                <p>
                    Incorrect. This value is the total probability that a file gets flagged by the filter, <m>P(B)</m>. You must use this number as your denominator in Bayes' Theorem to find the conditional probability <m>P(A \mid B)</m>.
                </p>
            </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
            <statement>
                <m>0.0132</m>
            </statement>
            <feedback>
                <p>
                    Incorrect. This value is the joint probability of the intersection <m>P(A \cap B) = P(B \mid A)P(A) = 0.0132</m>. Remember to divide this numerator by the total probability space <m>P(B)</m>.
                </p>
            </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
            <statement>
                <m>0.9400</m>
            </statement>
            <feedback>
                <p>
                    Incorrect. This value is the false positive rate condition, <m>P(B \mid A^c) = 1 - P(B^c \mid A^c) = 0.94</m>. This represents the probability of a safe file being flagged, not a flagged file being malicious.
                </p>
            </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
            <statement>
                <m>0.0236</m>
            </statement>
            <feedback>
                <p>
                    Incorrect. This result stems from a minor arithmetic carrying error when computing the intersection components. Double-check your decimals during the summation phase.
                </p>
            </feedback>
        </choice>
    </choices>
    <hint>
        <p>
            You need to set up Bayes' Theorem: <m>P(A \mid B) = \frac{P(B \mid A)P(A)}{P(B)}</m>. Before calculating, look closely at your given values: you are given <m>P(B^c \mid A^c) = 0.06</m>, which means its conditional complement is <m>P(B \mid A^c) = 1 - 0.06 = 0.94</m>. Use that to find <m>P(B)</m> first.
        </p>
    </hint>
    <solution>
        <p>
            To evaluate the conditional probability <m>P(A \mid B)</m>, we utilize the conditional base rules and apply Bayes' Theorem:
        </p>
        <p>
            1. <term>Find the prior and conditional complements:</term>
            <ul>
                <li><p>The probability that a file is safe (<m>A^c</m>): <m>P(A^c) = 1 - P(A) = 1 - 0.44 = 0.56</m></p></li>
                <li><p>The conditional probability that a safe file is flagged (<m>B \mid A^c</m>): <m>P(B \mid A^c) = 1 - P(B^c \mid A^c) = 1 - 0.06 = 0.94</m></p></li>
            </ul>
        </p>
        <p>
            2. <term>Calculate the total probability space for the denominator, <m>P(B)</m>:</term>
            Using the Law of Total Probability:
            <md>
                <mrow>P(B) \amp = P(B \mid A)P(A) + P(B \mid A^c)P(A^c)</mrow>
                <mrow>P(B) \amp = (0.03)(0.44) + (0.94)(0.56)</mrow>
                <mrow>P(B) \amp = 0.0132 + 0.5264 = 0.5396</mrow>
            </md>
        </p>
        <p>
            3. <term>Apply Bayes' Theorem to find the posterior probability:</term>
            <me>P(A \mid B) = \frac{P(B \mid A)P(A)}{P(B)} = \frac{0.0132}{0.5396} \approx 0.02446</me>
            Rounding to four decimal places yields <m>0.0245</m>.
        </p>
    </solution>
</exercise>



  </exercises>



